From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:44:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windows.networking.sockets: MessageWebSocket as Windows has
 it, measured case by case.

React Native in Office (AugLoop) keeps its service connection on a
MessageWebSocket, and reads its state from the socket's events rather
than from return values, so this follows Windows through every path a
probe on Windows could drive, line by line:

- The control's defaults (binary, whole messages, no size limit, 65536
  bytes outbound, a 30 second pong interval), the protocols asked for
  and the one chosen, request headers and credentials; the handshake
  carries Cache-Control: no-cache as Windows' does.
- The information and the output stream exist from the start and stay
  the same objects; the information has the local address and the
  protocol once connected.
- Each write is one message, text or binary as the control says at the
  time, and writes go out in the order they were made, one frame at a
  time: they used to run on the thread pool side by side over winhttp's
  single frame buffer.  IMessageWebSocket3 sends a message in frames.
- A message arrives whole unless the receive mode is PartialMessage;
  its DataReader is the same object each time, and its input stream
  reads it again from the start.
- Close sends the close frame, IClosable an empty one; afterwards
  writing and connecting fail at once with RO_E_CLOSED, and Closed comes
  with the server's answer, or 1006 when the connection ends without
  one.  A status no close frame may carry, or a reason past 123 bytes of
  UTF-8, is E_INVALIDARG and changes nothing.  A close frame from the
  server raises Closed and leaves this side free to write.
- A connection that ends with no close frame, or a server breaking the
  protocol, raises no Closed: a last MessageReceived carries the error,
  0x80072efe or 0x80072f78, which asking for its data returns without
  touching the out pointer; writes are accepted and fail with
  0x80072efe; Close sends nothing and raises Closed with 1006 before it
  returns.
- A server that will not upgrade fails the connection with 0x8019xxxx,
  its status; only ws and wss addresses are taken.
- WebSocketError reads an error as Windows.Web.WebError does.

The header order on the wire is the one difference left: Windows'
MessageWebSocket writes its handshake itself, and winhttp puts Host and
the key where it puts them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit c25d94c836d3325609c46658206f3280118b0046)

